1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:09,056
This is MATH 239, video 10a. The topic is compositions. This video uses the Sum, Product and String Lemmas from video 9.

2
00:00:09,506 --> 00:00:23,345
After this video, you can define a composition and its length and size. You can write a set of compositions with the star operation. You can count all compositions of n. And you can find the generating series when the parts or the length have restrictions.

3
00:00:23,795 --> 00:00:35,869
Here is the problem. In how many ways can we write n as an ordered sum of positive integers? The picture shows n equals 6 as six unit cells.

4
00:00:36,319 --> 00:00:48,812
Cut the row of cells into consecutive pieces. Here the cuts give 3, then 1, then 2. This list is a composition. Its entries are the parts. Each part is a positive integer.

5
00:00:49,262 --> 00:00:58,132
The length of a composition is the number of parts. Here the length is 3. The size is the sum of the parts. Here the size is 6.

6
00:00:58,582 --> 00:01:13,721
The order of the parts matters. 3, 1, 2 and 1, 2, 3 are different compositions. There is exactly one composition with no parts. It is the empty composition, epsilon, and its size is 0.

7
00:01:14,171 --> 00:01:19,094
Here are all compositions of 5, sorted by length. Each block is one part.

8
00:01:19,544 --> 00:01:22,376
Length 1: only the composition 5.

9
00:01:22,826 --> 00:01:28,353
Length 2: four compositions. 4 1, 1 4, 3 2 and 2 3.

10
00:01:28,803 --> 00:01:31,357
Length 3: six compositions.

11
00:01:31,807 --> 00:01:37,984
Length 4: four compositions. Each has three parts equal to 1 and one part equal to 2.

12
00:01:38,434 --> 00:01:42,799
Length 5: one composition, five parts equal to 1.

13
00:01:43,249 --> 00:01:53,466
The total is 16, which is 2 to the 4. The counts by length are the binomial coefficients 4 choose k. The next slides prove both facts with generating series.

14
00:01:53,916 --> 00:02:03,482
Let P be the set of positive integers. One part of a composition is one element of P. P to the 0 has one element: the empty composition.

15
00:02:03,932 --> 00:02:07,044
P to the 1 holds the compositions with one part.

16
00:02:07,494 --> 00:02:14,692
A composition of length k is a list of k elements of P. So it is an element of the Cartesian product P to the k.

17
00:02:15,142 --> 00:02:19,554
P to the 3 holds the compositions with three parts, and so on.

18
00:02:20,004 --> 00:02:34,725
The length can be any natural number. So the set C of all compositions is the union of all P to the k. This union is P star. The union is disjoint, because each composition has exactly one length.

19
00:02:35,175 --> 00:02:40,934
Now the generating series. The weight of a composition is its size, the sum of its parts.

20
00:02:41,384 --> 00:02:53,458
One part is an element of P, with weight equal to its value. So phi of P is x plus x squared plus x cubed, and so on. By the geometric series, this is x over 1 minus x.

21
00:02:53,908 --> 00:03:03,475
The size of a list is the sum of the sizes of its parts. So the Product Lemma gives: phi of P to the k equals x over 1 minus x, to the power k.

22
00:03:03,925 --> 00:03:07,408
These are the compositions of length k, weighted by size.

23
00:03:07,858 --> 00:03:20,954
C is P star, and P has no element of weight 0. So the String Lemma applies. Phi of C equals 1 over 1 minus x over 1 minus x. The orange row is 1 minus phi of P. Phi of C is its inverse.

24
00:03:21,404 --> 00:03:40,667
Multiply top and bottom by 1 minus x. This gives 1 minus x over 1 minus 2 x, which also equals 1 plus x over 1 minus 2 x. The green row shows its coefficients. Check the product at x cubed: 4 minus 2 minus 1 minus 1 is 0, as it must be.

25
00:03:41,117 --> 00:03:52,402
Here is the String Lemma as a picture. Row k is phi of P, to the power k. Row 0 is the series 1. It holds only the empty composition.

26
00:03:52,852 --> 00:04:07,527
Row 1 is x over 1 minus x. Row 2 is its square. The purple cell marks the first nonzero coefficient. Row k starts at x to the k, because each of the k parts has size at least 1.

27
00:04:07,977 --> 00:04:18,611
Cell n of row k counts the compositions of n with length k. Row 3, column 5 shows 6. This is the length-3 column of the last slide.

28
00:04:19,061 --> 00:04:26,120
Row 4 and row 5 continue the pattern. The entries are binomial coefficients: n minus 1 choose k minus 1.

29
00:04:26,570 --> 00:04:45,146
The String Lemma adds all the rows. Column n of the sum counts all compositions of size n. The sum row is 1, 1, 2, 4, 8, 16. So the number of compositions of n is 2 to the n minus 1, for n at least 1. For n equal to 0, it is 1.

30
00:04:45,596 --> 00:04:54,280
Check column 5. The rows give 0, 1, 4, 6, 4 and 1. The total is 16, the same as the list of all compositions of 5.

31
00:04:54,730 --> 00:05:06,573
Here is a direct, bijective proof. Write n as n unit cells in a row. Between the cells there are n minus 1 gaps. For n equal to 5, there are 4 gaps.

32
00:05:07,023 --> 00:05:14,128
In each gap, we cut or we do not cut. Each gap has 2 choices, so there are 2 to the n minus 1 choices in total.

33
00:05:14,578 --> 00:05:23,262
Each set of cuts gives one composition, and each composition gives one set of cuts. Cuts at gaps 2 and 3 give the parts 2, 1 and 2.

34
00:05:23,712 --> 00:05:37,087
A composition of length k has k minus 1 cuts. So there are n minus 1 choose k minus 1 such compositions. This matches the rows of the table. The notes prove this bijection in Proposition 2.23.

35
00:05:37,537 --> 00:05:49,812
Most exam questions restrict the compositions. The notes give a method in three steps. Step 1. Find the allowed values for one part. This gives a set P and its series phi of P.

36
00:05:50,262 --> 00:06:03,173
Step 2. Find the allowed lengths. If any length is allowed, the set is P star. If the length must be even, the set is P squared, star. If the length is exactly k, the set is P to the k.

37
00:06:03,623 --> 00:06:13,050
Step 3. Apply the Sum, Product and String Lemmas, and name each lemma in your written solution. The next slides use this method on the examples of the notes.

38
00:06:13,500 --> 00:06:26,039
Example 2.18. Let F be the set of compositions with each part 1 or 2. Step 1: P is the set 1, 2. Its series is x plus x squared.

39
00:06:26,489 --> 00:06:36,473
Step 2: any length is allowed, so F is P star. The left side shows the 5 compositions of 4 in F. The orange blocks are parts equal to 2.

40
00:06:36,923 --> 00:06:45,840
Step 3: the String Lemma gives phi of F equals 1 over 1 minus x minus x squared. Phi of P has no constant term, so the lemma applies.

41
00:06:46,290 --> 00:07:02,126
Expand the series. The coefficients are 1, 1, 2, 3, 5, 8, 13. The coefficient of x to the 4 is 5, which matches the list. Chapter 4 finds an exact formula for these numbers.

42
00:07:02,576 --> 00:07:16,740
Example 2.19. Let H be the set of compositions with each part at least 2. The allowed parts are 2, 3, 4, and so on. Their series is x squared over 1 minus x.

43
00:07:17,190 --> 00:07:28,661
Any length is allowed, so the String Lemma gives 1 over 1 minus x squared over 1 minus x. Clear the inner fraction. The result is 1 minus x, over 1 minus x minus x squared.

44
00:07:29,111 --> 00:07:36,448
This also equals 1 plus x squared over 1 minus x minus x squared. The denominator is the same as for F.

45
00:07:36,898 --> 00:07:48,973
Example 2.20. Let J be the set of compositions with each part odd. The allowed parts are 1, 3, 5, and so on. Their series is x over 1 minus x squared.

46
00:07:49,423 --> 00:07:54,206
The String Lemma gives 1 minus x squared, over 1 minus x minus x squared.

47
00:07:54,656 --> 00:07:59,671
This equals 1 plus x over 1 minus x minus x squared. Again the same denominator.

48
00:08:00,121 --> 00:08:05,601
So F, H and J have almost the same generating series. The next slide shows what this means.

49
00:08:06,051 --> 00:08:11,717
Here are the coefficient rows. Row h n counts compositions with parts at least 2.

50
00:08:12,167 --> 00:08:15,232
Row j n counts compositions with odd parts.

51
00:08:15,682 --> 00:08:18,933
Row f n counts compositions with parts 1 or 2.

52
00:08:19,383 --> 00:08:39,445
Each row is a shift of the f row. Phi of H is 1 plus x squared times phi of F. Phi of J is 1 plus x times phi of F. So for n at least 2, h n equals j n minus 1, equals f n minus 2. The three green cells are all equal to 8.

53
00:08:39,895 --> 00:08:51,412
Multiplication by x squared moves every coefficient two places to the right. So the sets H n, J n minus 1 and F n minus 2 have the same size. We proved this without a formula for the numbers.

54
00:08:51,862 --> 00:09:01,382
Here are the three sets for n equal to 7. Each has 8 compositions. Exercise 2.17 asks for bijections between them.

55
00:09:01,832 --> 00:09:17,018
Example 2.22. Let Q be the set of compositions with each part at least 2 and an even number of parts. Step 1 is the same as for H: phi of P is x squared over 1 minus x.

56
00:09:17,468 --> 00:09:30,889
Step 2. An even length is 2 j. Group the parts in pairs. A composition of length 2 j is a list of j pairs, so it is an element of P squared, to the j. So Q is P squared, star.

57
00:09:31,339 --> 00:09:47,314
Step 3. The Product Lemma gives phi of P squared. The String Lemma then gives 1 over 1 minus phi of P squared. This is 1 minus x squared, over 1 minus x squared minus x to the 4. Here the squares are of 1 minus x.

58
00:09:47,764 --> 00:09:58,074
Check with n equal to 7. The compositions are 2 5, 5 2, 3 4 and 4 3. There are 4, and the coefficient of x to the 7 is 4.

59
00:09:58,524 --> 00:10:14,592
Practice problem P3-5. Fix p at least 2. Count the compositions of n where every part is 1 or p. Pause the video and try it. Step 1: the set of parts is 1 and p. Its series is x plus x to the p.

60
00:10:15,042 --> 00:10:23,866
Any length is allowed, so the set is P star. The picture shows the case p equal to 3 and n equal to 7. There are 9 such compositions.

61
00:10:24,316 --> 00:10:41,127
The String Lemma gives the answer: the coefficient of x to the n in 1 over 1 minus x minus x to the p. For p equal to 3, the coefficients are 1, 1, 1, 2, 3, 4, 6, 9. The coefficient of x to the 7 is 9.

62
00:10:41,577 --> 00:11:00,803
Practice problem P3-6. The weight here is the length, not the size. Pause the video and try it. The parts are 1, 2 and 3. Each part adds 1 to the length, so each part has weight 1. Phi of P is 3 x.

63
00:11:01,253 --> 00:11:08,266
Here are the compositions of length 2. Each part has 3 choices, so there are 9. Each has weight 2.

64
00:11:08,716 --> 00:11:12,802
This agrees with the Product Lemma: 3 x squared is 9 x squared.

65
00:11:13,252 --> 00:11:27,881
The String Lemma gives 1 over 1 minus 3 x. The coefficients are 3 to the k. With the size as the weight, the same set gives 1 over 1 minus x minus x squared minus x cubed. Always check which weight the question uses.

66
00:11:28,331 --> 00:11:37,340
Recap. A composition is a list of positive integers. Its length is the number of parts. Its size is the sum of the parts.

67
00:11:37,790 --> 00:11:48,611
All compositions form P star. The String Lemma gives 1 minus x over 1 minus 2 x. So there are 2 to the n minus 1 compositions of n, for n at least 1.

68
00:11:49,061 --> 00:11:57,977
The compositions of n with length k number n minus 1 choose k minus 1. Choose k minus 1 cuts among n minus 1 gaps.

69
00:11:58,427 --> 00:12:06,415
For a restricted set, find the allowed parts and their series. Then find the allowed lengths. Then apply the lemmas and name them.

70
00:12:06,865 --> 00:12:18,150
Any length gives 1 over 1 minus phi of P. Even length gives 1 over 1 minus phi of P squared. Length exactly k gives phi of P to the k.

71
00:12:18,600 --> 00:12:26,588
Always check the weight: size or length. Video 10b solves the tutorial, assignment and midterm problems on compositions.
